G_BAD_REQ_LEN;` |\n| `app/src/rng.c:106-110` | `handleErrors()` → `abort()` (only on an OpenSSL EVP failure; unreachable for well-formed 32-byte keys) |\n| `app/src/rng.c:205` | `if (provided_data != NULL)` — behavioural branch, not an error return |\n| `app/src/sign.c:179-181` | `crypto_sign_verify`: `if (siglen != SPX_BYTES) return -1;` |\n| `app/src/sign.c:235-237` | `crypto_sign_verify`: `if (memcmp(root, pub_root, SPX_N)) return -1;` |\n| `app/src/sign.c:271-276` | `crypto_sign_open`: `if (smlen < SPX_BYTES) { memset(m,0,smlen); *mlen = 0; return -1; }` |\n| `app/src/sign.c:279-284` | `crypto_sign_open`: verify failed → `memset(m,0,smlen); *mlen = 0; return -1;` |\n\nThere are **no** `assert()` calls, no `RETURN_ERROR` macro, and no `return NULL`\nanywhere in the library. `#error` directives exist only in the params/backend\nheaders (`SPX_WOTS_W` not in {16,256}; `SPX_D` not dividing `SPX_FULL_HEIGHT`;\n`SPX_N > 32` with BLAKE-256/SHA-256; `SPX_WOTS_LEN2` not precomputed) — those are\ncompile-time, cannot be reached at runtime, and all 48 shipped configurations\nsatisfy them, so they get no runtime row.\n\nReturn-code constants (`app/include/rng.h`):\n`RNG_SUCCESS = 0`, `RNG_BAD_MAXLEN = -1`, `RNG_BAD_OUTBUF = -2`,\n`RNG_BAD_REQ_LEN = -3`.\n\n## Error-surface table\n\nEvery row has a differential test in `tests/errors.rs`\n(`err_<n>_...`). \"expected C result\" is the value the C `.so` actually\nreturns, established by calling it — not inferred from docs.\n\n| # | function | trigger (exact invalid input/condition) | expected C result | [ ] |\n|---|----------|------------------------------------------|-------------------|-----|\n| 1 | `seedexpander_init` | `maxlen == 0x100000000` (first value `>= 2^32`) | returns `-1` (`RNG_BAD_MAXLEN`); `*ctx` left **untouched** | [ ] |\n| 2 | `seedexpander_init` | `maxlen == 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF` (far above `2^32`) | returns `-1`; `*ctx` untouched | [ ] |\n| 3 | `seedexpander_init` | `maxlen == 0xFFFFFFFF` (largest accepted, one below the range check) | returns `0` (`RNG_SUCCESS`); `ctx->ctr[8..12] = FF FF FF FF`, `buffer_pos = 16` | [ ] |\n| 4 | `seedexpander_init` | `maxlen == 0` (zero length) | returns `0`; `length_remaining = 0`, `ctr[8..12] = 00 00 00 00` | [ ] |\n| 5 | `seedexpander` | `x == NULL` (null output pointer), `xlen` arbitrary | returns `-2` (`RNG_BAD_OUTBUF`); `ctx` untouched (checked *before* `length_remaining`) | [ ] |\n| 6 | `seedexpander` | `x == NULL` **and** `xlen >= ctx->length_remaining` — both faults at once; the null check is first | returns `-2`, **not** `-3` | [ ] |\n| 7 | `seedexpander` | `xlen == ctx->length_remaining` (equal, so the `>=` fires) | returns `-3` (`RNG_BAD_REQ_LEN`); `ctx->length_remaining` **not** decremented | [ ] |\n| 8 | `seedexpander` | `xlen > ctx->length_remaining` | returns `-3`; `ctx` untouched | [ ] |\n| 9 | `seedexpander` | `xlen == ctx->length_remaining - 1` (largest accepted) | returns `0`; output + `ctx` state must match byte-for-byte | [ ] |\n| 10 | `seedexpander` | `xlen == 0` with `length_remaining > 0` | returns `0`; `while(xlen>0)` never runs, so `ctx` unchanged except `length_remaining -= 0` | [ ] |\n| 11 | `seedexpander` | `xlen == 0` with `length_remaining == 0` → `0 >= 0` | returns `-3` | [ ] |\n| 12 | `seedexpander` | called on a ctx whose `length_remaining` was exhausted by an earlier successful call | returns `-3` | [ ] |\n| 13 | `crypto_sign_verify` | `siglen == SPX_BYTES - 1` (one below valid) | returns `-1` | [ ] |\n| 14 | `crypto_sign_verify` | `siglen == SPX_BYTES + 1` (one above valid) | returns `-1` | [ ] |\n| 15 | `crypto_sign_verify` | `siglen == 0` | returns `-1` | [ ] |\n| 16 | `crypto_sign_verify` | `siglen == SIZE_MAX` (oversized length) | returns `-1` | [ ] |\n| 17 | `crypto_sign_verify` | correct `siglen`, but one bit flipped in the `R` prefix (`sig[0..N]`) → wrong digest → root mismatch | returns `-1` | [ ] |\n| 18 | `crypto_sign_verify` | correct `siglen`, one bit flipped inside the FORS part of the signature | returns `-1` | [ ] |\n| 19 | `crypto_sign_verify` | correct `siglen`, one bit flipped inside a WOTS part | returns `-1` | [ ] |\n| 20 | `crypto_sign_verify` | correct `siglen`, one bit flipped inside a Merkle auth-path part | returns `-1` | [ ] |\n| 21 | `crypto_sign_verify` | valid signature verified against a **different** public key | returns `-1` | [ ] |\n| 22 | `crypto_sign_verify` | valid signature, message truncated by 1 byte (`mlen-1`) | returns `-1` | [ ] |\n| 23 | `crypto_sign_verify` | valid signature, one message byte flipped | returns `-1` | [ ] |\n| 24 | `crypto_sign_verify` | valid signature over `mlen == 0`, verified with `mlen == 0` | returns `0` (empty message is legal) | [ ] |\n| 25 | `crypto_sign_verify` | all-zero signature buffer of the correct `siglen` | returns `-1` | [ ] |\n| 26 | `crypto_sign_open` | `smlen == SPX_BYTES - 1` (one below the minimum) | returns `-1`, writes `*mlen = 0`, and zeroes exactly `smlen` bytes of `m` | [ ] |\n| 27 | `crypto_sign_open` | `smlen == 0` | returns `-1`, `*mlen = 0`, zeroes 0 bytes of `m` | [ ] |\n| 28 | `crypto_sign_open` | `smlen == SPX_BYTES` exactly (boundary; zero-length message) | returns `0`, `*mlen = 0` | [ ] |\n| 29 | `crypto_sign_open` | `smlen >= SPX_BYTES` but the signature part is corrupted → inner `crypto_sign_verify` fails | returns `-1`, `*mlen = 0`, and zeroes `smlen` bytes of `m` (note: **`smlen`**, not `smlen - SPX_BYTES`) | [ ] |\n| 30 | `crypto_sign_open` | valid `sm`, but verified under a different `pk` | returns `-1`, `*mlen = 0`, `m[0..smlen]` zeroed | [ ] |\n| 31 | `crypto_sign_open` | `smlen` one *greater* than the signed length (extra trailing byte appended) | returns `-1` (message length feeds the digest) | [ ] |\n| 32 | `AES256_CTR_DRBG_Update` | `provided_data == NULL` — takes the `if (provided_data != NULL)` false branch, so `temp` is **not** XORed in | `Key`/`V` updated from the raw AES output only | [ ] |\n| 33 | `AES256_CTR_DRBG_Update` | `provided_data != NULL` (4